"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[21774],{21774:e=>{e.exports="# Regression\n\nFor either linear or non-linear regression after the model was trained compute the residuals (differences) between predictions and labels, and verify the residuals are distributed as a zero mean normal distribution.\n\n## Linear Regression\n\nIf you are asked to train a linear regression or you reason that is the goal, try to figure out which of the three cases apply. If you are not sure, prompt the user for the input.\n\n### Model Selection\n\n- Use Spark MLlib Linear Regression for distributed, large-scale datasets that exceed single-machine memory. Set convergence tolerance and max iterations explicitly—defaults can stop too early. Enable L2 or elastic-net regularization for ill-conditioned features. Cache DataFrames before training to avoid repeated recomputation.\n- Use PyTorch Linear Model (torch.nn.Linear) for flexible prototyping, GPU access and custom control. Initialize weights with small random values (e.g., Xavier uniform). Use MSE loss; start with Adam optimizer for smooth convergence. Watch for vanishing updates if learning rates are too low. Use deterministic seeding for reproducibility across workers.\n- Use Scikit-learn LinearRegression with Ridge/Lasso for small- to medium-sized datasets, or parameter sanity checks before scaling out. Use Ridge for numerically unstable designs (high collinearity). Use Lasso for variable selection or sparsity. Check solver choice—saga works best with large or sparse data.\n\n### Regularization\n\nTo stabilize training consider use one of the following regularizations:\n\n- L2 (Ridge)\n- L1 (Lasso)\n- Elastic Net: Combination of both\n\n### Training Best Practices\n\nUse random initialization centered around zero to help estimators avoid symmetry traps. Learning rate should err on the side of small and get increased gradually. Consider batch shuffling across epochs to ensure workers see varied data partitions. Use early stopping or convergence tolerance instead of fixed epochs when possible. Track loss consistently with MLflow or similar to notice plateau or divergence early.\n\n## Non-linear Regression\n\nIf you are asked to train a non-linear regression or you reason that is the goal, then if the amount of data is sufficiently large train a deep neural network for heteroscedastic non-linear regression using samples {x_i, y_i}, where x_i ∈ ℝ^m is the input vector and y_i ∈ ℝ^n is the target vector. If the amount of data is small use PolynomialFeatures + LinearRegression from Scikit-learn for nonlinear regression.\n\n### Neural network\n\nFinal layer has n + 1 neurons: first n model mean vector μ(x_i), last neuron models log variance σ\xb2(x_i) -> softplus(\xb7) + ε (ensures positivity). This yields isotropic heteroscedastic Gaussian likelihood y_i ∼ \uD835\uDCA9(μ(x_i), σ\xb2(x_i) I_n). NLL loss: L = [1/(2σ\xb2(x_i))] ‖y_i - μ(x_i)‖\xb2 + (n/2) log σ\xb2(x_i).\nInitialize weights with small random values centered at zero (Xavier/He initialization). Use ReLU, Leaky ReLU, or ELU activations with batch normalization (preferred) or layer normalization to prevent vanishing/exploding gradients.\nFreeze variance neuron for first n_freeze epochs (warm-up). Add L2 regularization, dropout, or both. Stop training via patience on validation NLL (no improvement for set epochs).\n"}}]);
